# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene in Luke. Luke 18:9 opens: “And he spake this parable unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others.” Luke names no place and no time, and he does not say that the scene changes after 18:8. The hearers are “certain,” and Luke says two things of them: they “trusted in themselves that they were righteous,” and they “despised others.” Luke does not say who they are. He does not call them Pharisees, and he does not say whether the disciples, the Pharisees or others were among them.'))
A(('p', '2.2 What comes before. The account before it begins, “And he spake a parable unto them to this end, that men ought always to pray, and not to faint” (18:1), and it closes with a question: “Nevertheless when the Son of man cometh, shall he find faith on the earth?” (18:8). Luke 18:9 begins a new account with “And he spake this parable.” The word “pray” stands in both accounts, but Luke gives each its own hearers: “unto them” in 18:1 and “unto certain” in 18:9. Earlier in the same part of Luke, the text says Jesus “was demanded of the Pharisees, when the kingdom of God should come” (17:20), and that He “said unto the disciples” of days to come (17:22). Luke does not say that 18:9 continues that conversation.'))
A(('call', 'Luke Names the Hearers',
   'Luke gives the hearers in 18:9 as “certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others.” These are Luke’s words about them, not an explanation from Jesus. No question or remark from a hearer comes before the parable.'))
A(('p', '2.3 What comes after. The next account in Luke begins, “And they brought unto him also infants” (18:15). Luke does not say that the infants are connected to the two men or that the account answers the parable, and this study does not say so either.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. The parable stands in the ministry in which Jesus said, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (Luke 4:43). Matthew says that “Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). The parable itself does not use the word kingdom. It is told about two men who went up into the temple, and the Lord’s Kingdom ministry is the setting in which it stands.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives the reason in one verse: the parable was spoken to those who trusted in themselves that they were righteous and despised others (18:9). Luke does not give a question or an event that prompted it. He names what the hearers trusted and how they looked on others, and he does not say that Jesus had a particular person in mind.'))
A(('tbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Luke 18:9', '“certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others”', 'Luke’s words about the hearers.'],
    ['Luke 18:1', '“a parable unto them to this end, that men ought always to pray”', 'The account before it, with its own hearers.'],
    ['Luke 18:8', '“shall he find faith on the earth?”', 'The question that closes the earlier account.'],
    ['Luke 18:15', '“they brought unto him also infants”', 'The next account in Luke.'],
    ['Luke 17:20', '“demanded of the Pharisees, when the kingdom of God should come”', 'A conversation in the chapter before.'],
    ['Luke 4:43', '“I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also”', 'What Jesus says He was sent to do.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it. The quotations keep the KJV word “publican.”'))
A(('p', '3.1 Luke’s introduction (18:9). “And he spake this parable unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others.” Before the story begins, Luke tells the reader two things about the hearers: what they trusted and how they looked on others. These are Luke’s words, and they are kept apart from Jesus’ own words in Section 4.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The two men (18:10). “Two men went up into the temple to pray; the one a Pharisee, and the other a publican.” The verse has three steps. Two men go up. They go up into the temple. They go up to pray. The text names each man by one word, Pharisee and publican, and gives no other name. It does not say at what hour they went or whether either saw the other come.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The Pharisee’s prayer (18:11–12). “The Pharisee stood and prayed thus with himself, God, I thank thee, that I am not as other men are, extortioners, unjust, adulterers, or even as this publican. I fast twice in the week, I give tithes of all that I possess.” The sequence has five steps. He stands. He prays “with himself.” He thanks God. He says he is not as other men are and names “extortioners, unjust, adulterers,” and “this publican.” He says that he fasts twice in the week and gives tithes of all that he possesses. The text says what he says. It does not say that the fasting or the tithing was false, and it does not say they were wrong. It says “with himself” and does not say more about it.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The publican’s prayer (18:13). “And the publican, standing afar off, would not lift up so much as his eyes unto heaven, but smote upon his breast, saying, God be merciful to me a sinner.” The sequence has four steps. He is “standing afar off.” He “would not lift up so much as his eyes unto heaven.” He smites upon his breast. He says one sentence: “God be merciful to me a sinner.” The text does not say what he had done, what his work was, or whether he had wronged anyone or been wronged. It does not say that he offered a sacrifice, made a payment or did anything after he spoke.'))
A(('p', '3.5 What follows the prayers (18:14). Jesus does not end the story at the second prayer. The next verse begins, “I tell you, this man went down to his house justified rather than the other.” These are Jesus’ own words about the story. They are set out word for word in Section 4 and are not paraphrased here.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The two prayers side by side. The prayers are laid out together below so that each is read in its own words.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Point', 'The Pharisee (18:11–12)', 'The publican (18:13)'],
    ['How he is placed', '“stood”', '“standing afar off”'],
    ['His eyes', 'Not mentioned.', '“would not lift up so much as his eyes unto heaven”'],
    ['What he does', '“prayed thus with himself”', '“smote upon his breast”'],
    ['How he speaks to God', '“God, I thank thee”', '“God be merciful to me”'],
    ['What he says of himself', '“I fast twice in the week, I give tithes of all that I possess”', '“a sinner”'],
    ['What he says of others', '“not as other men are, extortioners, unjust, adulterers, or even as this publican”', 'Nothing is said of others.'],
], [0.20, 0.42, 0.38]))
A(('p', '3.7 What the story leaves unsaid. The text names the two men as a Pharisee and a publican and says nothing more of who they are. Jesus does not identify either man as standing for a group of people. He does not say what the temple is, what the going up and the going down are, or what the fasting, the tithes, the standing afar off or the blow upon the breast are. The story gives two prayers and then stops, and the one thing said after it is what Jesus says in 18:14.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Luke’s words and Jesus’ words. Luke’s words in 18:9 are the narrator’s description of the hearers. They are not an explanation of the story by Jesus and are kept apart from His words below.'))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus says after the prayers. In 18:14 Jesus says: “I tell you, this man went down to his house justified rather than the other: for every one that exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.” These are His own words, set out one part at a time.'))
