A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name. Jesus gives no explanation of the story beyond 18:14. He does not identify the Pharisee, the publican, the temple, the going up or the going down. He does not identify the fasting, the tithes, the standing afar off, the eyes lifted or not lifted, or the blow upon the breast. He does not say what “justified” includes or how a man is justified. For each of these the sentence is the same: Jesus does not identify it.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only the man who went down justified, the other man, the one who exalts himself and the one who humbles himself, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the Pharisee is a certain people, that the publican is a certain people, that the temple or the going up stands for something, or that the prayers are a pattern for being saved is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The explanation stays with its account. The words of 18:14 belong to this parable. A sentence with nearly the same words stands in Luke 14:11, in another account, and the word “justified” stands in other places in Luke. They are not read into this verse, and this verse is not read into them.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It repeats none of Jesus’ words from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 What kind of story it is. The parable does not open with “the kingdom of heaven is like.” It opens with two men who went up to pray, and Luke gives its purpose by naming what the hearers trusted and how they looked on others (18:9). The two prayers are set side by side, and the meaning of the comparison lies in the contrast between them.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    'Two men come to the same place for the same purpose, and they pray in very different ways.',
    'One prayer is made of thanks for what the man is and does and of words about other men by name. The other is made of a single plea for mercy.',
    'The first prayer is shaped by what Luke says of the hearers, trust in themselves and contempt for others. The second is shaped by what the man says of himself.',
    'The comparison turns on what each man says of himself and of others. Fasting, tithing and standing in the temple are mentioned, and the text does not say they are wrong.',
    'The force of the comparison is a reversal, which Jesus states in His own words in 18:14. This section adds no further reversal.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. Luke says the parable was spoken to those who trusted in themselves that they were righteous and despised others (18:9). They were meant to hear a warning aimed at the habit Luke names, trust in oneself and contempt for others, and not at the acts of praying, fasting or giving. The text does not say that the hearers were the Pharisee of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The parable and the kingdom. The parable does not say that it is about the kingdom. It was told in the ministry in which Jesus preached the kingdom, and the account after it speaks of the kingdom of God in its own words (18:16–17). This section does not join the two.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What this section does not do. It does not say who the Pharisee or the publican stands for. It does not say what the temple, the going up or the going down is. It does not turn “justified” into a doctrine or say how any person is justified. It does not say that the Pharisee’s fasting and tithing are wrong, and it does not say that the publican’s prayer is a form of words that earns anything.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The Pharisee and the publican stand in Luke 18. They are kept apart here from three neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 7:41–43', '“a certain creditor which had two debtors”', 'The Two Debtors. Told in a Pharisee’s house about a creditor, two debtors and a forgiving, with Simon’s answer to a question about love. It has no temple, no prayer and no going down justified.'],
    ['Luke 17:7–10', '“which of you, having a servant plowing or feeding cattle”', 'The Unprofitable Servants. A servant who has done what was commanded and a master who does not thank him. It has no temple, no prayer and no two men set side by side.'],
    ['Luke 18:1–8', '“a judge, which feared not God, neither regarded man”', 'The widow, read in the study of the Friend and the Persistent Widow. Told just before this parable, “unto them,” about a judge and a widow, with its own purpose stated in 18:1.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. This parable is told “unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others,” with no place named (18:9). The Two Debtors is told in a house, to a man named Simon, after Simon “spake within himself” (7:36–40). The servant of Luke 17 stands in a chapter that opens with words to the disciples (17:1), and the widow of Luke 18 is told “unto them” (18:1).'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. This parable has a Pharisee and a publican. The Two Debtors has a creditor and two debtors. The servant story has a servant and a master. The widow story has a judge and a widow.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. This parable moves from a going up, to two prayers, to one verdict. The Two Debtors moves from a debt, to a forgiving, to a question about love. The servant story moves from work, to a meal, to a saying about duty. The widow story moves from a refusal, to a persistence, to a granting.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the sentence about exalting and humbling stands in Luke 14:11, after a story told to guests who chose the chief rooms (14:7), and in Matthew 23:12, spoken to the multitude and to the disciples (23:1). Each stands in its own place. Second, the word “justified” stands in Luke 7:29, where the people and the publicans “justified God,” and in Luke 16:15, where Jesus says “Ye are they which justify yourselves before men.” Each is a different use, in a different setting, and none explains 18:14. Third, the account of the infants (18:15–17) and the ruler (18:18) follow this parable in Luke and are not carried back into it.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. In Luke, Jesus speaks “unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others” (18:9). The parable belongs to the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The two men and their prayers were told before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Luke 18:9–14 speaks of the Body of Christ, and the passage is not read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The two prayers, the temple, the fasting, the tithes, the standing afar off and the going down justified are not a description of the Body of Christ. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person is justified, and Jesus’ word “justified” in 18:14 is not carried over as Paul’s teaching to the Body. The going up, the standing, the fasting, the giving, the blow upon the breast and the plea are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Pharisee’s tithing is not a requirement for the Body, and the publican’s sentence is not a form of words the Body must say. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this story: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparison shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of self-examination.** The hearers trusted in themselves that they were righteous. A reader learns to ask what he trusts when he comes to pray.',
    '**A lesson about contempt.** The Pharisee names other men and the publican in his prayer. A reader learns to notice when his words about God carry words against others.',
    '**A lesson of honesty in prayer.** The publican says one thing of himself and asks for mercy. A reader learns to pray without setting himself beside another man.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** Jesus says that every one that exalteth himself shall be abased and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted (Luke 18:14). A reader learns to weigh how he regards himself.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the Pharisee, that the Body is the publican, or that the Body is the one who went down justified. They are principles drawn from what the story shows.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells of two men who went up into the temple to pray, a Pharisee and a publican. The Pharisee stood and prayed with himself, thanking God that he was not as other men are, naming extortioners, the unjust, adulterers and the publican, and saying that he fasts twice in the week and gives tithes of all that he possesses. The publican stood afar off, would not lift up so much as his eyes unto heaven, smote upon his breast and said, “God be merciful to me a sinner.” Jesus says that this man went down to his house justified rather than the other, and that every one that exalteth himself shall be abased and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted (Luke 18:14). Luke says the parable was spoken to certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous and despised others (18:9). Jesus names nothing else in the story. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the Pharisee or the publican stands for, that says what “justified” includes, or that makes going up, standing afar off, fasting, tithing or a prayer a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** two men go up into the temple to pray; the Pharisee prays with himself and the publican prays standing afar off; Jesus says what follows.',
    '**The meaning:** a prayer made of trust in oneself and words against others is set beside a plea for mercy, and the comparison turns on what each man says of himself and of others.',
    '**The boundaries:** only the man who went down justified, the other man, the one who exalts himself and the one who humbles himself are named; the Pharisee, the publican, the temple and the prayers are not identified.',
    '**The present lesson:** self-examination, no contempt for others, honest prayer and humility, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

