A(('h1', S13))
A(('p', '13.1 Related passages. Read each in its own setting and keep it apart from this parable.'))
A(('bul', [
    'Luke 18:1–8: the judge and the widow, told just before this parable with its own hearers and purpose.',
    'Luke 18:15–18: the infants and the ruler that follow, each with its own words.',
    'Luke 14:7–11: the guests who chose the chief rooms, and a sentence with nearly the same words as 18:14.',
    'Matthew 23:1–12: words spoken to the multitude and to the disciples, ending with a like sentence.',
    'Luke 7:29–30 and Luke 16:14–15: other places where the word “justified” stands, each in its own setting.',
    'Luke 5:27–32 and Luke 15:1–2: other accounts in Luke with publicans, each with its own words.',
    'Luke 4:43 and Matthew 4:17: what Jesus was sent to preach and what He preached.',
]))
A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Two Debtors, the Unprofitable Servants and the Friend and the Persistent Widow are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. This parable makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus tells of two men who went up into the temple to pray. One, a Pharisee, stood and prayed with himself and thanked God that he was not as other men are, and said that he fasts twice in the week and gives tithes of all that he possesses. The other, a publican, stood afar off, would not lift up so much as his eyes unto heaven, smote upon his breast and said, “God be merciful to me a sinner.” Jesus says that this man went down to his house justified rather than the other, and that every one that exalteth himself shall be abased and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.'))
A(('pk', 'Write the synthesis in your own words in five to seven sentences. In it:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Tell the two prayers in their order.',
    'State exactly what Luke says in 18:9 and what Jesus says in 18:14, and nothing beyond them.',
    'Say plainly which parts of the story Jesus does not identify.',
    'State what the comparison says about trust in oneself, contempt for others and a plea for mercy.',
    'State when Jesus told it and what He was sent to preach, and one principle of conduct for today.',
    'End with one sentence naming what the passage does not say.',
]))
A(('call', 'Boundaries to Remember',
   'Jesus names the man who went down justified, the other man, the one who exalts himself and the one who humbles himself, and nothing more. No identity for the Pharisee, the publican, the temple or the prayers. No symbol carried in from another parable. Going up, standing, fasting, tithing and praying are actions in a story, not steps of salvation. The Body of Christ is neither man. The lessons are principles of conduct, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
